  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "When denoting the genotype at a single locus, we refer to the pair of alleles that a (diploid) individual carries at the locus. Individuals that have two identical alleles are called homozygotes, whereas those that have different alleles are heterozygotes. If, for example, we denote the alleles at a particular diallelic locus as $ B_{1} $ and $ B_{2} $, there are three possible genotypes: $ B_{1}B_{1} $ and $ B_{2}B_{2} $ homozygotes, and $ B_{1}B_{2} $ heterozygotes. There may, of course, be more than two alleles, and hence more than three genotypes, present at a locus.",
      "source_page": 68,
      "printed_page": 48,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter4:p002:b5",
        "Genetics_chapter4:p002:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        156.0,
