        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "9ade07796e30472f6a1ff2c24c56907e981f242b5e9bc92abdcad16cb3ac3a55"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "$ B_{1}B_{2} $, and $ B_{2}B_{2} $. If these are the only possible genotypes at the locus, then by definition, $ P_{11} + P_{12} + P_{22} = 1 $. If there are N individuals in the population, then $ P_{11}N $ individuals contain two $ B_{1} $ alleles and $ P_{12}N $ individuals contain a single $ B_{1} $ allele. Since there are a total of 2N genes in the population for each autosomal locus, the frequency of the $ B_{1} $ allele is simply",
      "source_page": 69,
      "printed_page": 49,
